# agent-bridge monorepo conventions

This repo is a pnpm workspace (`apps/*`, `packages/*`). Follow these conventions strictly.

## 1. Never hand-edit dependency blocks

Always add/remove/upgrade deps via `pnpm` commands. Never edit `dependencies` / `devDependencies` / `peerDependencies` blocks in any `package.json` by hand.

```bash
# add to a specific workspace
pnpm add <pkg> --filter <workspace-name>
pnpm add -D <pkg> --filter <workspace-name>

# add a root devDep (e.g. tooling)
pnpm -w add -D <pkg>

# workspace-to-workspace dep (e.g. backend depends on @agent-bridge/shared)
pnpm add @agent-bridge/shared --filter <workspace-name> --workspace

# remove
pnpm remove <pkg> --filter <workspace-name>
```

`<workspace-name>` is the `name` field of the target package's `package.json`
(e.g. `backend`, `frontend`, `worker`, `@agent-bridge/shared`, `@agent-bridge/db`).

Commit the updated `pnpm-lock.yaml` together with every dep change.

## 2. Shared-code rule

If a type, Zod schema, constant, or utility is used by **2+ workspaces**, it lives in `packages/*`. Duplicating types between `apps/backend` and `apps/frontend` is a bug.

- Zod/DTO types, enum constants, SSE event envelope types, canvas layout types → `packages/shared` (browser-safe entry, `.`).
- Node-only helpers (env parsing, crypto, paths, spawn, gitnexus) → `packages/shared` under **subpath exports** so the browser bundle never pulls them in.
- DB schema + queries → `packages/db` (`@agent-bridge/db`), consumed by `apps/backend` and `apps/worker`.
- Agent builder (Mastra wiring, memory, tools) → `packages/agents` (`@agent-bridge/agents`), consumed by backend and mcp-bridge.

## 3. Subpath exports for runtime boundaries

`@agent-bridge/shared` is the one package that both browser and Node import. Keep them separated via `exports`:

```jsonc
// packages/shared/package.json
{
  "exports": {
    ".":         { /* browser-safe: types, zod, DTO */ },
    "./env":     { /* Node-only */ },
    "./crypto":  { /* Node-only */ },
    "./paths":   { /* Node-only */ },
    "./spawn":   { /* Node-only */ },
    "./gitnexus":{ /* Node-only */ }
  }
}
```

Frontend code may only import from `@agent-bridge/shared` (the `.` entry). Importing from a Node-only subpath into the browser bundle will break the build — that's intentional.

## 4. New workspaces

`pnpm-workspace.yaml` already globs `apps/*` and `packages/*`. Creating a new workspace is: make the folder, add `package.json` (private, workspace name), add `tsconfig.json` extending `tsconfig.base.json`. Then `pnpm install` picks it up automatically.

## 5. Isolation — nothing leaks onto the user's machine

All runtime writes go through `@agent-bridge/shared/paths` (`AGENT_BRIDGE_DATA_DIR`, default `<repo>/.agent-bridge-data/`). Every `gitnexus` / `git` child process goes through `@agent-bridge/shared/spawn`'s `spawnSandboxed`, which clamps `HOME` to the isolated data dir. Never spawn `gitnexus` via `npx` — use `runGitnexus()` from `@agent-bridge/shared/gitnexus` (resolved-path spawn against the pinned version).

## 6. Secrets at rest

Any user-supplied secret (LLM API key, MCP env var, MCP HTTP header, git PAT) is stored as an encrypted envelope via `encryptSecret()` from `@agent-bridge/shared/crypto`. Plaintext never leaves the backend — API responses return `{set: true, length}` sentinels, never the value. Before streaming anything over SSE, run it through `redactSecrets()`.
